57
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:01,470
Now it looks like the Houthis might have
added

58
00:03:01,531 --> 00:03:04,171
another tactic to their attempts to
disrupt global commerce.

59
00:03:04,771 --> 00:03:08,693
They were recently accused of cutting four
underwater internet cables in the Red Sea.

60
00:03:08,893 --> 00:03:11,593
The four cables cut were AAE-1,

61
00:03:11,713 --> 00:03:16,195
and AAE stands for Asia, Africa, and
Europe, which is

62
00:03:17,215 --> 00:03:21,636
a 15,535-mile system that connects East
Asia to Europe through Egypt

63
00:03:22,036 --> 00:03:25,617
and China to the West through Pakistan and
Qatar. EIG,

64
00:03:25,637 --> 00:03:28,618
that stands for Europe-India Gateway,
which links

65
00:03:28,779 --> 00:03:32,701
Europe to Africa and Asia and connects Southern
Europe to the Middle East and India.

66
00:03:33,102 --> 00:03:34,563
The third cable was Seacom,

67
00:03:34,583 --> 00:03:37,405
which is owned by the South African
company of the same name,

68
00:03:37,925 --> 00:03:39,766
which connects Europe, Africa, and Asia.

69
00:03:40,827 --> 00:03:45,110
And then the fourth one was TGN, which is
a major link between India and the West.

70
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:49,733
SECOM execs have said that it will take at
least two months to repair their cable,

71
00:03:49,933 --> 00:03:52,535
and just like with commercial shipping
transiting the region,

72
00:03:52,795 --> 00:03:56,598
the cost will be much higher than
normal because of the increased insurance premiums

73
00:03:56,718 --> 00:03:59,540
due to current hazards associated with
operating in the Red Sea.

74
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:01,681
Hong Kong telecoms company

75
00:04:01,981 --> 00:04:05,884
HGC Global Communications announced the
disruption earlier this week.

76
00:04:06,184 --> 00:04:09,366
As is to be expected based on the regions
these cables serve,

77
00:04:09,526 --> 00:04:13,829
the cuts worsened internet connections in
India, Pakistan, and East Africa.

78
00:04:14,209 --> 00:04:16,110
Internet providers have rerouted traffic,

79
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,072
but because those lines are already being
heavily used,

80
00:04:19,372 --> 00:04:23,015
doing that doesn't return customers' bandwidth to where
it was with the Red Sea lines.

81
00:04:23,335 --> 00:04:24,256
And just like shipping,

82
00:04:24,436 --> 00:04:27,960
the relatively shallow Red Sea is a major
passageway for the Internet,

83
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,185
with more than 15 undersea cables connecting
Europe, Asia, and the Middle East.

84
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:34,347
A Wired magazine feature back in

85
00:04:34,467 --> 00:04:39,373
2022 called the Red Sea the Internet's
most vulnerable place on Earth. These days,

86
00:04:39,393 --> 00:04:43,196
when we think about internet access, we
automatically equate that with Wi-Fi,

87
00:04:43,276 --> 00:04:45,778
and we conceive of that as something that
travels through space.

88
00:04:45,958 --> 00:04:50,522
But undersea cables are the cheapest and
fastest way to send data, and as a result,

89
00:04:50,882 --> 00:04:55,026
they account for 99% of internet traffic
between continents, according to CNET.

90
00:04:55,246 --> 00:04:57,648
And the New York Times reports that
Google, Facebook,

91
00:04:57,708 --> 00:05:00,110
and Microsoft have been running new cable
systems,

92
00:05:00,471 --> 00:05:01,872
which can cost up to $350 million each.

93
00:05:03,453 --> 00:05:07,995
The cables are typically 16 slim fiber
optic strands that transmit the data,

94
00:05:08,295 --> 00:05:12,156
surrounded by a layer of copper armoring
to protect and stabilize the strands.

95
00:05:12,516 --> 00:05:15,377
The core is then encased in a polyethylene
jacket.

96
00:05:15,738 --> 00:05:18,519
This lightweight cable about the
circumference of a human thumb

97
00:05:18,799 --> 00:05:22,640
runs through the deepest parts of the ocean,
where it's unlikely to be disturbed by

98
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:25,201
activities like boat anchoring and fishing
operations.

99
00:05:25,621 --> 00:05:28,802
As the cable travels into shallower water
that sees more traffic,

100
00:05:28,983 --> 00:05:32,584
it's armored with additional casings that
can reach about two inches in diameter.

101
00:05:33,084 --> 00:05:37,451
Laying a cable is a years-long process
which costs millions of dollars. First,

102
00:05:37,491 --> 00:05:39,914
companies look at naval charts to plot the
best route.

103
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:44,461
Cables are safest in deep water where they
can rest on a relatively flat seabed

104
00:05:44,661 --> 00:05:47,545
and won't rub against rocks or be at risk
of other disturbances.

105
00:05:48,106 --> 00:05:51,269
Once the route is plotted and checked and
the shore connections are secure,

106
00:05:51,609 --> 00:05:53,891
huge cable-laying ships begin loading the
cable,

107
00:05:53,911 --> 00:05:57,774
which can take up to a month depending on
the number of spools required. Of course,

108
00:05:57,814 --> 00:06:03,439
communicating between continents using undersea cables
isn't new. On July 29, 1858,

109
00:06:03,599 --> 00:06:06,902
two steam-powered battleships met in the
middle of the Atlantic Ocean

110
00:06:07,302 --> 00:06:11,286
and connected two ends of a
2,500-mile-long, half-inch-wide cable,

111
00:06:11,606 --> 00:06:13,888
which linked Europe and North America by
telegraph.

112
00:06:14,168 --> 00:06:16,629
The first message between UK's Queen
Victoria and

113
00:06:16,729 --> 00:06:20,170
US President James Buchanan took over 17
hours to deliver

114
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:24,591
at 2 minutes and 5 seconds per letter by
Morse code. In 1956,

115
00:06:24,651 --> 00:06:27,512
transatlantic number one, known as TAT-1,

116
00:06:27,752 --> 00:06:30,973
the first underwater telephone cable was
laid, and by 1988,

117
00:06:31,093 --> 00:06:35,534
TAT-8 was transmitting 280 megabytes per
second. about

118
00:06:35,815 --> 00:06:37,517
15 times the speed of an average U.S.

119
00:06:37,557 --> 00:06:40,040
household internet connection over fiber
optics,

120
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:43,764
which use light to transmit data at
breakneck speeds. Today,

121
00:06:43,804 --> 00:06:47,108
there are around 380 underwater cables in
operation around the world,

122
00:06:47,408 --> 00:06:48,870
spanning a length of over 745,645 miles.

123
00:06:52,074 --> 00:06:55,596
Damage to these cables isn't necessarily
rare and can be caused by

124
00:06:55,676 --> 00:07:00,039
natural disasters like the storm that took
out the internet in Taiwan in 2006

125
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,163
and Hurricane Sandy in 2012 that cost an
estimated $71 billion to repair

126
00:07:05,423 --> 00:07:08,225
and knocked out several key exchanges
where undersea cables

127
00:07:08,325 --> 00:07:09,686
linked North America and Europe.

128
00:07:10,086 --> 00:07:13,569
But two-thirds of cable failures are
caused by accidental human activities,

129
00:07:13,929 --> 00:07:16,210
fishing nets and trawling, and also ships'
anchors.

130
00:07:16,611 --> 00:07:18,572
The rest of the human disruptions are
malicious.

131
00:07:18,952 --> 00:07:23,095
And what researcher Nicole Starosielski
describes in her book The Undersea Network

132
00:07:23,275 --> 00:07:24,857
as security by obscurity,

133
00:07:25,077 --> 00:07:27,579
meaning groups can't target something they
don't know about,

134
00:07:27,979 --> 00:07:29,960
is no longer a sound defense strategy.

135
00:07:30,341 --> 00:07:33,443
Undersea cables have also been the target
of spycraft over the years.

136
00:07:33,863 --> 00:07:35,404
During the Cold War, U.S.

137
00:07:35,444 --> 00:07:39,365
submarines transported divers with specially designed
equipment that they attached to

138
00:07:39,465 --> 00:07:41,866
Soviet cables to intercept all
communications.

139
00:07:42,266 --> 00:07:44,546
The secret surveillance lasted almost a
decade

140
00:07:44,626 --> 00:07:47,887
until information about the operation,
codenamed Ivy Bells,

141
00:07:48,107 --> 00:07:49,648
was sold to the Soviets by a

142
00:07:49,768 --> 00:07:53,689
former National Security Agency communications
specialist named Ronald Pelton.

143
00:07:54,049 --> 00:07:58,371
And in 2013, documents provided by
National Security Agency whistleblower

144
00:07:58,491 --> 00:08:02,733
Edward Snowden exposed that the British
spy agency GCHQ had

145
00:08:02,853 --> 00:08:05,295
secretly gained access to the network of
cables

146
00:08:05,495 --> 00:08:08,057
which carried the world's phone calls and
internet traffic.

147
00:08:08,358 --> 00:08:11,320
According to the documents provided by
Snowden, in 2012,

148
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:14,902
GCHQ was handling 600 million telephone
events every day

149
00:08:15,203 --> 00:08:17,925
and had compromised more than 200 fiber
optic cables.

150
00:08:18,345 --> 00:08:21,308
The NSA allegedly ran a similar operation
called Upstream,

151
00:08:21,469 --> 00:08:24,472
which a presentation leaked by Snowden
described as being

152
00:08:24,632 --> 00:08:27,015
able to access communications on fiber
cables and

153
00:08:27,135 --> 00:08:31,780
infrastructure as data flows past. In a
statement, an NSA spokesman said the agency

154
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:34,583
can neither confirm nor deny
mission-related activities.

155
00:08:34,903 --> 00:08:38,227
But if you control the cable itself, you
don't need to worry about tapping it.

156
00:08:38,467 --> 00:08:40,949
Chinese telecoms giant Huawei began moving

157
00:08:41,009 --> 00:08:45,313
into the undersea cable market a few years
ago. And in 2017, Australia blocked

158
00:08:45,394 --> 00:08:49,617
a plan for the company to install a 2,485
mile undersea cable

159
00:08:49,838 --> 00:08:51,559
linking Sydney with the Solomon Islands.

160
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:55,143
The Chinese deny that their systems pose
an inherent security risk

161
00:08:55,323 --> 00:08:58,926
or that they have ulterior motives for
creating them. But back to the Red Sea.

162
00:08:59,286 --> 00:09:02,588
Although the Yemeni government in exile
and many other regional experts

163
00:09:02,748 --> 00:09:06,630
predicted cutting the internet cables on the Red Sea
floor would be something the Houthis would do,

164
00:09:06,910 --> 00:09:10,252
and the Israeli news outlet Globes has
directly blamed the Houthis,

165
00:09:10,612 --> 00:09:12,514
the Houthis have denied responsibility and

166
00:09:12,574 --> 00:09:16,836
claimed that the cables were cut by American
military ships as a red flag operation.

167
00:09:17,156 --> 00:09:19,357
Houthi leader Abdel Melek al-Houthi said,

168
00:09:19,377 --> 00:09:21,779
"...we have no intention of targeting sea
cables

169
00:09:21,859 --> 00:09:23,620
providing internet to countries in the
region."

170
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:27,661
Maybe a more important question than if
the Houthis did it is how they did it.

171
00:09:28,001 --> 00:09:28,542
As we said,

172
00:09:28,722 --> 00:09:31,983
major naval powers have the ability to cut
internet cables on the sea floor,

173
00:09:32,323 --> 00:09:35,764
but how would a third world power do it?
Of the four networks cut,

174
00:09:35,904 --> 00:09:37,645
only AAE-1 touches Yemen,

175
00:09:37,985 --> 00:09:40,306
and cutting the line there would only
disrupt service in that

176
00:09:40,366 --> 00:09:42,086
country and not the others that were
affected.

177
00:09:42,506 --> 00:09:45,767
So this operation would have to take place
at depth in the middle of the Red Sea.

178
00:09:46,088 --> 00:09:48,728
Even at the relatively shallow depth of
several hundred feet,

179
00:09:48,788 --> 00:09:51,310
that's a challenging task. In a recent
episode,

180
00:09:51,330 --> 00:09:55,132
we discussed that Operation Prosperity
Guardian warships had neutralized

181
00:09:55,272 --> 00:09:57,853
an unmanned subsurface vessel known as a
USV.

182
00:09:58,134 --> 00:10:01,155
Iran has a long tradition of
unconventional naval warfare,

183
00:10:01,256 --> 00:10:03,297
including uncrewed underwater drones,

184
00:10:03,517 --> 00:10:07,199
and it's reasonable to assume they provided
that capability to proxies like the Houthis.

185
00:10:07,519 --> 00:10:11,622
And in 2018, a video emerged of Houthi
divers with a captured U.S.

186
00:10:11,662 --> 00:10:13,903
Navy Remus 600 underwater drone.

187
00:10:14,123 --> 00:10:17,005
As with the technology behind their
surface-to-surface missiles,

188
00:10:17,265 --> 00:10:20,507
the Houthis could be more advanced than
perceived, and their USVs,

189
00:10:20,547 --> 00:10:22,268
including the captured Remus 600,

190
00:10:22,688 --> 00:10:25,370
might have been modified with the
components that give them the ability to

191
00:10:25,430 --> 00:10:29,292
find and cut cables on the sea floor. Or
maybe the merchant vessel Ruby Mars

192
00:10:29,392 --> 00:10:30,433
severed the cables when it

193
00:10:30,513 --> 00:10:34,235
settled on the bottom of the Red Sea after being
hit by a Houthi missile a few weeks ago,

194
00:10:34,495 --> 00:10:37,097
and then floating adrift for days before
finally sinking.

195
00:10:37,537 --> 00:10:40,619
The truth will come out eventually. And if
the Houthis are responsible,

196
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,242
then they've added to their list of
adversary countries, most notably China.

197
00:10:44,582 --> 00:10:47,345
The bottom line is that all of this is
netting out to a 42%

198
00:10:47,685 --> 00:10:50,507
drop in monthly transits through the Suez
Canal and an

199
00:10:50,947 --> 00:10:55,170
82% decrease in container tonnage from its
peak in 2023, according to the UN.

200
00:10:55,471 --> 00:10:56,992
So between that and the impact

201
00:10:57,172 --> 00:11:00,034
on internet connectivity on countries far
away from the Red Sea,

202
00:11:00,334 --> 00:11:01,695
consumers worldwide would be

203
00:11:01,796 --> 00:11:04,878
wise not to conceive of the Houthi threat
as something that doesn't affect them.

204
00:11:05,178 --> 00:11:07,483
More on this situation as the information
becomes available.

205
00:11:07,523 --> 00:11:09,127
So if you're not already a subscriber,

206
00:11:09,187 --> 00:11:11,773
become one so you don't miss anything. And
in the meantime,

207
00:11:12,113 --> 00:11:14,018
I look forward to talking to you again
very soon.

